1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,026
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:02,576 --> 00:00:09,742
This short briefing compares two complementary MAH governance systems: Interface Management and Stakeholder Management.

3
00:00:10,292 --> 00:00:20,477
We will look at how the Engineering and Construction Sector coordinates technical connection points on one side, and stakeholder relationships on the other, across the full delivery of a project.

4
00:00:22,177 --> 00:00:23,793
Here is how we will go through it.

5
00:00:24,343 --> 00:00:27,411
First, the core distinction between the two disciplines.

6
00:00:27,961 --> 00:00:30,727
Then their scope and structure, side by side.

7
00:00:31,277 --> 00:00:35,726
Next, the two roles compared directly, and where the two systems intersect.

8
00:00:36,276 --> 00:00:38,833
After that, why they sit in separate departments.

9
00:00:39,383 --> 00:00:44,273
We will close with the key takeaways and the controlled documents this comparison is based on.

10
00:00:45,973 --> 00:00:49,505
Both disciplines coordinate people, but they control different risks.

11
00:00:50,055 --> 00:00:54,679
Interface Management asks: what must connect, align or close?

12
00:00:55,229 --> 00:01:04,600
Its focus is boundaries, dependencies, design integration and field coordination at physical, functional and organizational connection points.

13
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:11,423
If it is ignored, we get clashes, handoff gaps, rework and unresolved technical dependencies.

14
00:01:11,973 --> 00:01:17,687
Stakeholder Management asks a different question: who can affect the project, and how should we engage them?

15
00:01:18,237 --> 00:01:25,775
Its focus is the influence, interest and expectations of the people and parties around the project, mapped on a Power-Interest Matrix.

16
00:01:26,325 --> 00:01:34,896
If it is ignored, we face resistance, non-response, conflicting expectations, reputational impact and pressure to deviate from policy.

17
00:01:36,596 --> 00:01:39,977
Here are the same five dimensions, answered two different ways.

18
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:46,427
The unit of analysis for interfaces is an interface point; for stakeholders, it is a stakeholder.

19
00:01:46,977 --> 00:01:58,752
Interfaces are split simply into internal and external points, while stakeholders are categorized on the Power-Interest Matrix: manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed, or monitor.

20
00:01:59,302 --> 00:02:08,953
Each system has its own core register, its own issue log, and its own owning role: the Interface Manager on one side, and the Stakeholder Manager on the other.

21
00:02:10,653 --> 00:02:12,710
Now the two roles compared directly.

22
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:21,634
The Interface Manager controls connection points: clashes, holding areas, and every place where scopes, systems and work zones meet.

23
00:02:22,184 --> 00:02:26,378
The key question is: where do our works meet, and is every point resolved?

24
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:34,268
The Stakeholder Manager controls relationships: the influence, interest and expectations of the people and parties around the project.

25
00:02:34,818 --> 00:02:39,070
The key question is: who matters, what do they expect, and are they on board?

26
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:50,083
The tools, records, success measures and governing procedures are different: PRO-001 for interfaces and PRO-002 for stakeholders.

27
00:02:50,633 --> 00:02:53,805
That is why these two roles cannot be merged into one title.

28
00:02:54,355 --> 00:03:00,279
The Interface Manager closes the technical point, and the Stakeholder Manager secures the agreement behind it.

29
00:03:01,979 --> 00:03:03,584
So where do they intersect?

30
00:03:04,134 --> 00:03:07,190
The answer is: they are connected, but not merged.

31
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:17,750
The Interface Manager keeps the Interface Points Register, the Interface Matrix, design and field coordination, and technical RFIs and clashes.

32
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:27,196
The Stakeholder Manager keeps the Stakeholder Register, the Power-Interest Matrix, the engagement and communication plan, and relationship-level conflicts.

33
00:03:27,746 --> 00:03:31,615
Each role keeps its own owner, register, RACI and procedure.

34
00:03:32,165 --> 00:03:39,911
The Interface Manager supports the Stakeholder Manager's communication flow, and both registers are aligned after every coordination meeting.

35
00:03:40,461 --> 00:03:54,198
Escalation also stays separate but aligned: interface conflicts follow the PRO-001 route, stakeholder disagreements follow the PRO-002 escalation matrix, and both feed the same governance chain.

36
00:03:55,898 --> 00:04:01,880
This leads to organizational separation: two disciplines, two departments, not one combined role.

37
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:11,059
Interface Management is a technical and engineering function, accountable for physical and design coordination across disciplines and contractors.

38
00:04:11,609 --> 00:04:17,695
Stakeholder Management is accountable for client, authority and stakeholder engagement and communication.

39
00:04:18,245 --> 00:04:34,804
Merging them carries a real risk: technical coordination gets diluted by negotiation, slowing clash resolution and design sign-off, while stakeholder engagement gets diluted by day-to-day technical firefighting, weakening client and authority relationships.

40
00:04:35,354 --> 00:04:42,462
The recommendation is clear: keep them as two separate departments, each with its own manager, register and RACI ownership.

41
00:04:44,162 --> 00:04:45,337
To summarize.

42
00:04:45,887 --> 00:04:57,604
First, different roles, different owners: the Interface Manager controls connection points, the Stakeholder Manager controls relationships, and each has its own register, matrix, RACI and procedure.

43
00:04:58,154 --> 00:05:07,224
Second, aligned, not combined: the two registers are aligned after every coordination meeting, giving two accountable owners and one coordinated picture.

44
00:05:07,774 --> 00:05:22,022
Third, escalate to the right depth: technical clashes go through the three-level interface escalation, while relationship, contractual or reputational issues go onto the six-level stakeholder matrix, up to Legal and Corporate Governance.

45
00:05:23,722 --> 00:05:42,405
This comparison is drawn from four controlled MAH documents: the Interface Management Policy and the Projects Interface Management Procedure, issued in October 2025, and the Stakeholder Management Policy and the Projects Stakeholder Management Procedure, issued in November 2025.

46
00:05:42,955 --> 00:05:49,529
All four were checked and approved by the same governance chain, up to the VP of the Engineering and Construction Sector.

47
00:05:50,079 --> 00:05:50,825
Thank you.

48
00:05:51,375 --> 00:05:53,676
The floor is now open for questions.

